1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:06,159
Same house. Same street. Same three bedrooms. Three versions of you buy it at three different moments.

2
00:00:06,479 --> 00:00:15,071
One pays one thousand five hundred and sixty three dollars a month. One pays eight hundred and nineteen. And one pays almost three thousand.

3
00:00:15,391 --> 00:00:17,223
For the exact same house.

4
00:00:17,543 --> 00:00:22,709
One of them spends years owing more than the house is worth. And yet the ending isn't what you'd expect.

5
00:00:23,509 --> 00:00:26,911
July two thousand six. Everyone you know is buying a house.

6
00:00:27,231 --> 00:00:33,860
Max just bought his second one. He says house prices only go up. The man at the bank agrees with him, enthusiastically.

7
00:00:34,180 --> 00:00:39,370
You find a nice house for three hundred thousand dollars. This is our example house. Remember it.

8
00:00:39,690 --> 00:00:41,849
Here the timeline splits into three.

9
00:00:42,649 --> 00:00:46,074
Timeline A buys right now, in July two thousand six.

10
00:00:46,394 --> 00:00:49,645
Timeline B rents and waits until February twenty twelve.

11
00:00:49,965 --> 00:00:55,561
Timeline C is a version of you buying the same house today, in November twenty twenty six.

12
00:00:55,881 --> 00:01:05,204
Each puts down twenty percent and takes a thirty year fixed mortgage at the real average rate of that month. The house's value follows a real national home price index.

13
00:01:06,004 --> 00:01:11,229
Timeline A. You pay sixty thousand dollars down and borrow two hundred and forty thousand.

14
00:01:11,549 --> 00:01:15,763
The average thirty year rate that month is six point seven nine percent.

15
00:01:16,083 --> 00:01:23,107
Your payment, principal and interest only: one thousand five hundred and sixty three dollars a month, for thirty years.

16
00:01:23,427 --> 00:01:27,061
You get the keys. You paint the kitchen. You feel like a real adult.

17
00:01:27,861 --> 00:01:30,740
And then the American housing market collapses.

18
00:01:31,060 --> 00:01:37,028
Two thousand seven, two thousand eight. Banks fail. Millions of homes go into foreclosure across the country.

19
00:01:37,348 --> 00:01:43,408
By February twenty twelve, the national home price index is down about twenty seven percent from the peak.

20
00:01:43,728 --> 00:01:48,523
Your three hundred thousand dollar house is now worth about two hundred and eighteen thousand.

21
00:01:48,843 --> 00:01:52,175
And you still owe the bank about two hundred and twenty three thousand.

22
00:01:52,975 --> 00:01:56,539
This is called being underwater. You owe more than the thing is worth.

23
00:01:56,859 --> 00:02:03,953
After five and a half years of payments and a sixty thousand dollar down payment, if you sold the house, you'd still owe the bank money.

24
00:02:04,273 --> 00:02:09,788
Max, the guy with two houses, is in much worse shape. He has to sell one at a big loss.

25
00:02:10,108 --> 00:02:16,470
You can't sell without losing everything you put in. So you do the only thing you can. You keep paying, and you keep living there.

26
00:02:17,270 --> 00:02:23,168
While timeline A is underwater, timeline B is getting teased at every family dinner.

27
00:02:23,488 --> 00:02:31,754
Still renting? You're throwing money away! says an uncle who has never once checked his own mortgage statement.

28
00:02:32,074 --> 00:02:37,450
But rent isn't throwing money away. It buys a roof, flexibility, and no repair bills when the boiler dies.

29
00:02:37,770 --> 00:02:44,248
Timeline B pays rent, saves the difference every month, and builds a down payment while prices fall.

30
00:02:44,568 --> 00:02:48,655
For a few years, it looks like doing nothing. It's actually waiting with a plan.

31
00:02:49,455 --> 00:02:54,226
Meanwhile, timeline B has been renting, and saving, and waiting. February twenty twelve.

32
00:02:54,546 --> 00:03:06,354
The same house is now on sale for about two hundred and eighteen thousand. And mortgage rates have dropped to three point eight seven percent.

33
00:03:06,674 --> 00:03:10,354
You put down forty three thousand five hundred and borrow about one hundred and seventy four thousand.

34
00:03:10,674 --> 00:03:16,851
Your monthly payment: eight hundred and nineteen dollars. Almost half of timeline A's payment, for the same house.

35
00:03:17,651 --> 00:03:21,110
Why such a difference? Two things changed at once. The price, and the interest rate.

36
00:03:21,430 --> 00:03:29,801
Mortgage rates move a lot. Over six and a half percent in two thousand six. Under four in twenty twelve. A record low around two point six five percent in January twenty twenty one.

37
00:03:30,121 --> 00:03:34,696
Then back up to almost eight percent in late twenty twenty three.

38
00:03:35,016 --> 00:03:36,281
On a thirty year loan, a few percentage points can change the total cost by hundreds of thousands of dollars.

39
00:03:37,081 --> 00:03:44,767
Back to timeline A, still underwater, still paying.

40
00:03:45,087 --> 00:03:53,191
Slowly, the market recovers. In January twenty seventeen, the national index finally climbs back above its two thousand six peak.

41
00:03:53,511 --> 00:04:00,186
Ten and a half years after you bought, your house is worth what you paid for it again.

42
00:04:00,506 --> 00:04:06,253
And then it keeps going.

43
00:04:07,053 --> 00:04:11,500
September twenty twenty six. The same house is now worth about five hundred and forty five thousand dollars, according to the index.

44
00:04:11,820 --> 00:04:15,930
Timeline A owes about one hundred and thirty four thousand. Its equity, the part of the house it really owns, is about four hundred and eleven thousand.

45
00:04:16,250 --> 00:04:20,325
Timeline B owes about one hundred and fourteen thousand. Its equity is about four hundred and thirty two thousand.

46
00:04:20,645 --> 00:04:27,750
Both did well. But B bought later, paid much less every month, and still ends up ahead.

47
00:04:28,070 --> 00:04:32,273
And remember, timeline A only got here because it never sold. The people who were forced to sell in twenty ten or twenty eleven locked in their losses forever.

48
00:04:33,073 --> 00:04:39,099
Now count what each one actually paid out of pocket, including the down payment.

49
00:04:39,419 --> 00:04:45,444
Timeline A: about four hundred and thirty eight thousand dollars so far.

50
00:04:45,764 --> 00:04:52,730
Timeline B: about one hundred and eighty seven thousand.

51
00:04:53,050 --> 00:05:01,746
To be fair, timeline B also paid rent for those five and a half years. But even with that, the gap is enormous.

52
00:05:02,066 --> 00:05:07,407
Same house. The only difference was when they bought, and the rate they got.

53
00:05:08,207 --> 00:05:10,958
And now, timeline C. You, today, in November twenty twenty six, looking at the same house.

54
00:05:11,278 --> 00:05:17,745
The price is about five hundred and forty six thousand. The average thirty year rate is seven point two one percent.

55
00:05:18,065 --> 00:05:20,933
Twenty percent down is over one hundred and nine thousand dollars. The loan is about four hundred and thirty six thousand.

56
00:05:21,253 --> 00:05:29,960
The monthly payment: two thousand nine hundred and sixty six dollars. More than three and a half times timeline B, for the same three bedrooms.

57
00:05:30,280 --> 00:05:35,772
And over thirty years, the interest alone would be about six hundred and thirty one thousand dollars.

58
00:05:36,572 --> 00:05:39,915
Here are all three payments side by side.

59
00:05:40,235 --> 00:05:45,193
Eight hundred and nineteen. One thousand five hundred and sixty three. Two thousand nine hundred and sixty six.

60
00:05:45,513 --> 00:05:51,213
Same house. Three completely different lives.

61
00:05:52,013 --> 00:05:54,753
A quick note for everyone outside the US.

62
00:05:55,073 --> 00:06:02,631
Thirty year fixed rates are very American. In many European countries, mortgages are fixed for only a few years, or move with interest rates.

63
00:06:02,951 --> 00:06:08,838
That means your payment can change while you live in the house. When rates jumped in twenty twenty two and twenty twenty three, many families saw their monthly cost rise sharply.

64
00:06:09,158 --> 00:06:15,264
So wherever you live, ask one question before you sign. What happens to my payment if rates go up two points?

65
00:06:16,064 --> 00:06:20,906
And one more trap. The bank will often approve you for more than you should borrow.

66
00:06:21,226 --> 00:06:25,905
Lenders look at whether you can make the payment. Not whether you can still save, travel, or survive losing your job.

67
00:06:26,225 --> 00:06:27,931
Timeline A felt rich on approval day. Five years later, the payment was eating the budget alive.

68
00:06:28,251 --> 00:06:35,589
A good test: if your income dropped for six months, could you still pay? If the answer is no, the house is too big for now.

69
00:06:36,389 --> 00:06:39,895
So what can you actually learn, if nobody can time the market?

70
00:06:40,215 --> 00:06:45,161
One. The monthly payment matters more than the price tag. Rates can change it by thousands a year.

71
00:06:45,481 --> 00:06:51,088
Two. Timeline A survived because it didn't have to sell. Buy only if you can stay put through a bad few years.

72
00:06:51,408 --> 00:06:57,121
Three. Max lost because he stretched for a second house with borrowed money. Leverage cuts both ways.

73
00:06:57,441 --> 00:07:04,418
And four. A house is first a place to live. If the numbers only work when prices keep rising, the numbers don't work.

74
00:07:05,218 --> 00:07:07,772
The honest part. This is not financial advice.

75
00:07:08,092 --> 00:07:15,430
This is one example house following a national average index. Local markets can be very different, in the US and in Europe.

76
00:07:15,750 --> 00:07:21,799
Taxes, insurance, maintenance and fees aren't included, and they can add a lot to the cost of owning.

77
00:07:22,599 --> 00:07:27,150
Same house. Three timelines. Eight hundred dollars a month, or three thousand.

78
00:07:27,470 --> 00:07:32,241
Tell me in the comments: when did you buy, or are you still waiting for the right moment?

79
00:07:32,561 --> 00:07:34,291
And I'll see you in the other timeline.

